---
/*
  /lab/chrome — a LOCAL-ONLY bench, dev-gated below, linked from nowhere.

  BOTH ENDS OF THE PUBLIC CHROME, ON ONE BENCH AND ON PURPOSE. It opened as
  /lab/header and grew the second half within the hour, at Michael's ask: *"can
  this also be a lab to test site footers at the same time?"* Yes, and the "at
  the same time" is the whole argument — judged apart you can pick a top and a
  bottom that contradict each other, and each of the three header proposals
  below makes a claim the footer can break outright. See THE PAIRINGS.

  THE FIRST QUESTION, Michael 2026-08-11: *"if I look at the main site header
  for the blog, I feel like it's a little bit generic."*

  He is right, and `design.md` already agreed in writing: §14's last line —
  "Custom favicon / wordmark" — is the one item there never struck through. The
  favicon half was answered 2026-07-30 (the drawn star, §8). The wordmark half
  never was.

  THE DIAGNOSIS THIS BENCH IS BUILT ON, in four parts:

    1. THE SHAPE IS THE STARTER-TEMPLATE SHAPE. A 56px sticky strip, translucent
       blurred ground, hairline bottom, wordmark left, three links right, a
       sun/moon at the end. Every piece is defensible alone; the arrangement is
       the thing §3 bans on sight — it could belong to anyone.

    2. THE WORDMARK IS THE SITE'S BEST ASSET, RENDERED AS A NAV LABEL. "It Only
       Happens Once" is a whole sentence and the site's whole thesis, set at
       16/18px, `leading-none`, `whitespace-nowrap`, in the slot where a logo
       goes. Newsreader carries optical sizing precisely so a display face can
       behave differently at masthead scale. The header never asks it to.

    3. ON /blog THERE ARE TWO MASTHEADS, STACKED. The bar, then 2rem below it
       the Writing · Quotes · Music switch at `text-3xl` — and both mark "where
       you are" the same way, with a `border-primary` underline
       (`SiteLayout.astro:103`, `blog/index.astro:232`). One idiom, two sizes,
       one directly above the other. The lower one is the good, editorial,
       specifically-his element; the bar reads as a weaker echo of the thing it
       is sitting on. This is most of what "generic" is pointing at, and it is
       the finding the whole bench is arranged around.

    4. AND THAT UNDERLINE IS ACCENT IN CHROME, which §5 forbids in the sentence
       that defines it: *"Accent color is for content, never for chrome … it
       never colors a label, an eyebrow, or a category tag."* An active-state
       marker is furniture.

  ⚠ WHAT IS NOT ON THE TABLE: THE STAR. §3's reserved-glyph rule keeps ✦ and
  `StarMark` for the constellations, and dropping one into the chrome is the
  obvious first idea. Its being banned is USEFUL rather than limiting — it
  forces every variant here to be made of structure and typography, which is
  what §3 says distinctiveness is made of anyway.

  ⚠ NOTHING SHIPPED IS TOUCHED — the law page-card set. Every variant is CSS
  over `SiteLayout`'s REAL header DOM, keyed off a `data-hx` attribute the
  script sets on <html>. So `current` is not a copy of the header, it IS the
  header with the stylesheet doing nothing, and there is no drift to police. The
  theme toggle, the colophon popover and the mobile menu all keep working here,
  because they are the shipped ones. The whole experiment deletes in one file.

  ⚠ A VARIANT IS A PROPOSITION ABOUT THE PAIR, NOT ABOUT THE BAR. Finding 3 is
  about the bar AND the page's own masthead together, so two of the three
  variants restyle the switch as well. Judging the bar on its own is exactly the
  habit that produced the stack.

  ⚠ THE TWO HALVES OF THIS BENCH ARE NOT BUILT THE SAME WAY, and they cannot
  be. The header EXISTS, so every variant of it is CSS over the shipped element
  and `current` is the real control. There is no footer at all, so every variant
  of it is new markup that exists nowhere else — the bench is the only place it
  has ever been rendered, and `none` is the real control. Nothing to drift from
  is the compensation for having nothing to restyle.

  THE FOOTER QUESTION IS NOT "WHAT GOES IN A FOOTER". A footer was removed in
  `c83c3d3` ("the redundant footer is removed"), and what it held was a hairline
  plus the wordmark repeated at 12px and 40% opacity — nothing. Deleting it was
  right and it settled nothing, because the site kept the two jobs a footer does
  and gave each of them somewhere else to live:

    · THE CLOSE already exists, in exactly ONE room. `ConstellationSuite`'s
      outro is a rule, `pt-16`, and a `text-4xl` display-face *"← return"*, and
      §13 states the law behind it: **a suite always resolves upward.** So
      `invitation` below is not an invention. It asks whether that law is about
      suites or about the site.

    · THE END MATTER already exists, in a POPOVER. `Colophon` holds the
      typefaces (with the sentence about why Atkinson), the stack, the no-AI
      attestation, the source, and — per plan 34 §5 — *the feed's only visible
      address*. All of it behind a 16px ⌗ in the site's most contested row.

      ⚠ AND THE ⌗ STAYS THERE. It is the one thing on this bench that was
      settled rather than tested (see the deleted variants below). The division
      of labour that follows from it is the whole footer design: the POPOVER is
      a drawer — opened on demand, reachable from every page including short
      ones, good at detail. The FOOTER is a terminus — reached only by
      finishing, and therefore good at two things a drawer is bad at: what to do
      next, and what you want found by someone who read to the end.

  ⚠ THE FOOTER RENDERS INSIDE <main>, one level off from where it would ship.
  Slot content lands in main and this bench does not get to fork the layout, so
  the footer is the last child of the slot rather than a sibling of it. Visually
  identical in both rooms here — main is full-bleed and both rooms overflow the
  viewport — with ONE thing it therefore cannot show you: on a SHORT page, a
  real footer is pushed to the bottom by the wrapper's `flex-1` and this one
  would float up under the content. Judge the footer's design here; judge its
  behaviour on a short page after it ships.

  THE HEADER IS DECIDED — `runninghead`, and it is now the GROUND rather than a
  variant. Michael, 2026-08-11, after driving four: *"for top bar definitely want
  to ditch everything but the runninghead (by far the best)."*

  So the bar recedes: no hairline, no blur, a solid ground, and one uniform
  typographic register across the whole line — the site's name set in the same
  serif small-caps as the links beside it, at the same size. The running head of
  a printed book, where the page you are on is the thing you read and the head
  above it is a courtesy. The page's own switch keeps its size AND its accent
  underline, and is the only masthead on screen. There is no radio group for the
  bar any more; every footer below is judged under this one.

  ⚠ `?v=current` STILL WORKS and is deliberately not in the panel. `current` was
  never CSS — it is the attribute set to a value no rule matches — so keeping the
  escape hatch costs one line of nothing and makes "is this actually better"
  answerable for as long as the shipped bar still exists. `masthead` and `spine`
  are gone; `git log -- src/pages/lab/chrome.astro` has them.

  THE NAV RENAME, same conversation: **Home → Constellations.**

  This reverses §13's recorded decision (*"The top bar carries an explicit Home
  item … so the way back to the site's strangest room is never in doubt"*) and it
  reverses it TOWARD that same section's other sentence: *"Visitors see
  **constellations**."* The doc has been holding both positions since July —
  chrome that says Home, prose that says constellations — and the word "Home" was
  the more generic of the two in the most-seen row on the site. `/constellations`
  already 301s to `/`, so the site has been calling that room by this name in its
  routing the whole time. **design.md §13 and §11 need the edit if this ships.**

  ⚠ IT IS RENAMED BY SCRIPT HERE, not in `SiteLayout`. CSS cannot change text,
  and the bench does not fork the layout — so one line rewrites the shipped
  link's `textContent`. Watch the width: "Constellations" is roughly 2.5× "Home",
  in a row that also holds a wordmark, two more links and two utilities.

  THE FOOTER — THE CONTENT IS SETTLED, ONLY THE ARRANGEMENT IS OPEN.

  Michael, same message: *"keep the site footer minimalistic and only has: say
  hello, rss with icon and Github icon with Github text (shows the same popover
  as in the top bar) … also want to put site links in the footer as well for good
  measure. the return button for footer is just bad."*

  So every variant below carries exactly the same five things and differs only in
  how they sit:

      Constellations · Blog · About        Say hello   ⌁ RSS   ⌗ GitHub

    none    as it ships — nothing. Kept as the control, because "does the page
            end badly" was never actually judged and it is still the first
            question. The sky ends on its last constellation; /blog on "Load
            older essays".

    row     ONE LINE. Site links left, the three utilities right, one register
            for all six (serif small-caps — the bar's own language, so the two
            ends of the page speak the same way). The most minimal reading of
            the brief.

    stack   TWO LINES, FLUSH LEFT. Links, then utilities beneath them. No right
            edge to fight with, and it degrades to itself on a phone instead of
            wrapping into one. Differs from `row` in LAYOUT only.

    chrome  ONE LINE, TWO REGISTERS. Same split as `row`, but the utilities drop
            to §7's chrome register (uppercase, sans, muted) while the links stay
            serif small-caps. Differs from `row` in REGISTER only — the claim
            being that destinations and instruments are different KINDS of thing
            and should not be set alike.

  ⚠ THE ⌗ IN THE FOOTER OPENS THE SHIPPED POPOVER — the same one, not a copy. It
  is a second `popovertarget="site-colophon"` trigger, which the platform allows
  natively. **And it exposes a real bug in shipped code, which is the most
  valuable thing this bench has found:** `Colophon`'s positioning script anchors
  to `document.querySelector('button[popovertarget="site-colophon"]')` — the
  FIRST such button in the document, i.e. always the top bar's. Press the footer
  one and the panel opens 2,000px away, off-screen at the top of the page.
  The bench repositions it locally so the idea can be judged at all; the shipping
  fix is one line in `Colophon.astro` — anchor to the button that was PRESSED
  (`e.target.closest(...)`) rather than to the first one on the page. Nothing
  here patches shipped code; the note is the deliverable.

  ⚠ AND FOUR FOOTER VARIANTS DIED TO GET HERE, under page-card's rule that a
  bench is legitimate while its question is live:

    · `invitation`, `provenance`, `addresses`, `reprise` — all four were built on
      the assumption that a footer's job is to CLOSE. Michael: *"the return
      button for footer is just bad."* He is right, and the reason is in the
      bench's own notes from an hour earlier: the suite already closes, and it
      closes upward. A site-wide "return" duplicates that in the one room where
      it works and invents a destination in the room where it does not (the sky
      had to turn its arrow around). A footer is not an ending. It is where the
      addresses live.
    · `colophon` (footer holds the end matter, ⌗ leaves the bar) and `machinery`
      (a bare service row) died earlier the same day — the first because the ⌗ is
      a credential rather than end matter and stays put, the second because it
      was the ordinary answer drawn to be rejected. What `machinery` was reaching
      for survives here: the difference is that the links now have a reason and
      the ⌗ opens the drawer instead of repeating it.
    · `provenance`'s idea — a commit SHA, the one credential a website builder
      cannot fake — is NOT dead, it is unplaced. It did not survive as a footer
      because it needs prose around it, and the footer Michael wants has no
      prose in it. If it lands anywhere it is the colophon drawer or a /colophon
      room. `VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA` is set on every build and still unread.

  THE TOGGLES (orthogonal — each is one CSS rule)

    sticky off   Does the bar need to follow you at all? `BackToTop` already
                 exists, and the Sky's return ✦ does this job at depth.
    align        Bar as a band across the window, or as the top line of the
                 page's own column. ⚠ THIS TOGGLE IS PARTLY A TRAP, and seeing
                 that is the point: the blog is `max-w-6xl` and the front door
                 is `max-w-4xl`, so one "aligned" bar cannot align to both.
                 Switch rooms with it on.

  WHAT TO JUDGE
    1. THE RENAME, FIRST AND WITHOUT SCROLLING. "Constellations" is now the
       longest word in the bar and it sits beside a wordmark that is a whole
       sentence. Does the row still read as a running head, or as a list?
    2. THE SAME RENAME AT 900px, where the bar is still horizontal and the
       space is at its worst. The burger arrives at 768.
    3. THE BOTTOM ON `none` FIRST. The honest question is not "which footer" but
       "does the page end badly".
    4. `row` → `chrome`. One register or two? This is the whole remaining
       question and it is a §7 question: the footer's links are destinations and
       its utilities are instruments, which §4 says belong to different rooms —
       but here they are in the same six inches of page.
    5. `row` → `stack` at full width, then narrow to a phone. `row` has to wrap
       somewhere; `stack` is already wrapped. Which one wraps better is decided
       at about 600px.
    6. PRESS THE FOOTER'S ⌗. The drawer should open upward, anchored to the
       button you pressed. Then press the bar's, to confirm they are one drawer
       and not two.
    7. BOTH THEMES, from the toggle in the bar (it is the real one).
    8. ⚠ ON /blog THE FOOTER IS BEHIND EVERY PAGE OF THE FEED. The real route
       appends pages as you scroll (`scripts/blog-feed.ts`) and only stops at
       `pageCount`, so on the site's busiest room the footer is reached last or
       never. This bench renders ONE page of seven, so it is ~2 screenfuls down
       here and much further there. That is an argument about what belongs in a
       footer: anything a reader NEEDS cannot only live there.
    9. THE SKY. It has no masthead of its own and its column is `max-w-4xl`
       while the footer is `max-w-6xl` — so the footer's rule will overhang the
       content it is closing. Same trap as the `align` toggle, other end of the
       page, and it is a real decision: the site has two measures and its chrome
       has to pick one.

  WHAT THIS BENCH CANNOT ANSWER
    • /about, the reference implementation (§3). Its first movement hangs a
      serif numeral in the left margin, and a two-line masthead landing on top
      of that is a real risk this bench does not show.
    • A constellation suite, where the landing header and the floating ✦ live
      under the same bar — AND which already has its own close. A site footer
      arriving under that outro would be the second ending in a row, and it is
      the one room where every variant here might simply be wrong.
    • A SHORT page, per the note above — and `/blog/[slug]` and `/about` both
      already end in end-matter of their own (an article footer offering the way
      back into the Index; the authorship attestation). A site footer has to
      survive landing under those, and neither is a room here.
    • The wordmark itself — §14's actual open item. Every variant here sets the
      SAME words in the SAME face and changes only their rank. Whether "It Only
      Happens Once" should be drawn, lettered, broken across lines or given a
      mark of its own is the next bench, and this one is upstream of it: rank
      first, then form.

  ⚠ THE ROOMS ARE THE REAL ROOMS AT REDUCED FURNITURE. The blog room renders
  real `PostCard`s over the real feed query and the real subject rail; the sky
  room renders real constellations through app.css's own `.sky-*` classes. What
  is missing is only what could not be reached without forking a page: the
  feed's infinite scroll, the sky's focus tracker and its `transition:name`
  morphs. None of them touch the top 200px, which is the whole subject here.
*/
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import PostCard from '../../components/PostCard.astro';
import Reader from '../../components/Reader.astro';
import StarBloom from '../../components/StarBloom.astro';
import Label from '../../components/Label.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import { attachNeighbourhoods, listSubjects, listWriting } from '../../lib/blog';
import { listConstellations } from '../../lib/constellations';
import { renderMarkdown } from '../../lib/markdown';

if (import.meta.env.PROD) return Astro.redirect('/', 302);

const supabase = Astro.locals.supabase;
const params = Astro.url.searchParams;
const room = params.get('room') === 'sky' ? 'sky' : 'blog';

/* Read back so a reload — or a room change, which IS a navigation — keeps the
   arrangement you were judging. The attributes themselves are set by the inline
   script below; these only pre-check the controls. There is no list for the bar
   any more: it is `runninghead` unless the URL says `?v=current`, and that hatch
   is read straight off `location.search` rather than round-tripped through a
   control nobody can see. */
const FOOTERS = [
  ['none', 'none — as it ships'],
  ['row', 'row — one line, one register'],
  ['stack', 'stack — two lines, flush left'],
  ['chrome', 'chrome — one line, two registers'],
] as const;
const footer = (FOOTERS.find(([f]) => f === params.get('f'))?.[0] ?? 'none') as string;
const unsticky = params.get('sticky') === 'off';
const measured = params.get('align') === 'measure';

/* Only the room on screen is queried. Both are real reads against the live
   corpus — a header judged over lorem is a header judged over nothing. */
const writing = room === 'blog' ? await listWriting(supabase, { page: 1 }) : null;
if (writing) await attachNeighbourhoods(supabase, writing.items);
const subjects = room === 'blog' ? await listSubjects(supabase, 'writing', { selected: [], q: null }) : [];
const refs = room === 'sky' ? await listConstellations(supabase) : [];

/* The site's three destinations, for the FOOTER's copy of them. `Home` is
   `Constellations` here — see the rename note in the header; the bar's own copy
   of this link is renamed by script, because that one is shipped markup. */
const SITE_NAV = [
  { label: 'Constellations', href: '/', room: 'sky' },
  { label: 'Blog', href: '/blog', room: 'blog' },
  { label: 'About', href: '/about', room: '' },
] as const;

/* The sky's ramp, copied from `index.astro` rather than imported — it is nine
   lines of local const in a page, not a module. The shipped page stays the
   source of truth; this is the bench standing under the same type scale so the
   bar is judged over the real rhythm. */
const TIERS = [
  'text-4xl sm:text-5xl',
  'text-3xl sm:text-4xl',
  'text-2xl sm:text-3xl',
  'text-xl sm:text-2xl',
  'text-lg sm:text-xl',
];
const STAR_TIERS = ['text-base', 'text-sm', 'text-sm', 'text-xs', 'text-xs'];
const GLOW_TIERS = [1, 0.82, 0.66, 0.5, 0.36];
const distinct = [...new Set(refs.map((r) => r.count))].sort((a, b) => b - a);
const tierIndex = (count: number) => Math.min(distinct.indexOf(count), TIERS.length - 1);
const INDENTS = ['', 'sm:pl-16', 'sm:pl-32'];
const indentOf = (i: number) => INDENTS[i % 3 === 2 ? 2 : i % 2 === 1 ? 1 : 0];

const SUBJECT_CAP = 8;
---

<SiteLayout title="Chrome — bench" active={room === 'sky' ? 'home' : 'blog'} backToTop={false}>
  {
    /* ⚠ SYNCHRONOUS AND FIRST, so the variant is on <html> before the browser
      paints — the header markup is already parsed by the time this runs, and no
      style has been resolved yet, so there is no flash of the shipped bar. It
      also hangs the three hooks the stylesheet needs on the SHIPPED elements,
      which is what lets the bench restyle a header it is not allowed to edit,
      and performs the one thing CSS cannot do: the Home → Constellations
      rename. */
  }
  <script is:inline>
    (() => {
      const p = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
      const r = document.documentElement;
      // The bar is decided; `?v=current` is the escape hatch, not the default.
      r.dataset.hx = p.get('v') ?? 'runninghead';
      r.dataset.hxF = p.get('f') ?? 'none';
      if (p.get('sticky') === 'off') r.dataset.hxSticky = 'off';
      if (p.get('align') === 'measure') r.dataset.hxAlign = 'measure';
      // The FIRST <header> in document order is SiteLayout's — it opens the
      // layout, before <main>. Nothing else on the page can be reached first.
      const bar = document.querySelector('header');
      bar?.classList.add('hx-bar');
      bar?.querySelector('a')?.classList.add('hx-name');
      const menu = document.getElementById('site-menu');
      menu?.classList.add('hx-nav');

      // Home → Constellations. The ONE thing on this bench that is not CSS:
      // text is content, and the shipped nav array lives in SiteLayout. The
      // real change is one string there; this is one string here so the width
      // it costs can be seen before anyone edits the layout.
      const home = menu?.querySelector('a[href="/"]');
      if (home) home.textContent = 'Constellations';
    })();
  </script>

  {
    room === 'blog' && writing && (
      <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-6xl px-5 py-8 sm:py-10">
        {/* The page's own masthead — /blog's switch, verbatim, plus the nav
            `spine` relocates into it. */}
        <nav
          class="hx-switch border-base-300 mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-7 gap-y-2 border-b"
          aria-label="Content"
        >
          <span
            aria-current="page"
            class="font-display border-primary text-base-content -mb-px border-b-2 pb-3 text-2xl leading-none font-light sm:text-3xl"
          >
            Writing
          </span>
          <a
            href="/blog?view=quotes"
            class="font-display text-base-content/35 hover:text-base-content/70 -mb-px border-b-2 border-transparent pb-3 text-2xl leading-none font-light sm:text-3xl"
          >
            Quotes
          </a>
          <span class="font-display text-base-content/20 -mb-px border-b-2 border-transparent pb-3 text-2xl leading-none font-light sm:text-3xl">
            Music <span class="align-middle font-sans text-xs tracking-wide">soon</span>
          </span>
        </nav>

        <div class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-[13rem_1fr] md:gap-12">
          <aside class="md:sticky md:top-20 md:self-start">
            <div class="relative">
              <Icon
                name="ph:magnifying-glass"
                class="text-base-content/35 pointer-events-none absolute top-1/2 left-3 -translate-y-1/2 text-base"
              />
              <input
                type="search"
                placeholder="Search writing…"
                aria-label="Search writing (inert on the bench)"
                autocomplete="off"
                class="rounded-field border-base-300 bg-base-100 placeholder:text-base-content/35 focus:border-base-content/30 w-full border py-2 pr-3 pl-9 font-sans text-sm focus:outline-none"
              />
            </div>

            {subjects.length > 0 && (
              <div class="mt-7">
                <Label variant="chrome">Subjects</Label>
                <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-1.5 md:flex-col md:gap-0.5">
                  {subjects.slice(0, SUBJECT_CAP).map((s) => (
                    <li>
                      <span class="border-base-300 text-base-content/55 md:rounded-field inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border px-2.5 py-0.5 font-sans text-xs">
                        {s.name}
                        <span class="text-base-content/30">{s.count}</span>
                      </span>
                    </li>
                  ))}
                </ul>
              </div>
            )}
          </aside>

          <section class="min-w-0">
            <div class="divide-base-300 divide-y">
              {writing.items.map((item) => (
                <PostCard item={item} />
              ))}
            </div>
          </section>
        </div>
      </div>
    )
  }

  {
    room === 'sky' && (
      <section class="mx-auto flex min-h-[80vh] max-w-4xl flex-col justify-center px-5 py-20 sm:px-8">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-12 sm:gap-16">
          {refs.map((c, i) => (
            <div class={indentOf(i)}>
              <a href={`/${c.slug}`} class:list={['group sky-row block', `cn-${c.color}`]}>
                <div class="flex items-baseline gap-3">
                  <span
                    class:list={['sky-star', STAR_TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]]}
                    style={`--glow: ${GLOW_TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]}`}
                  >
                    <StarBloom />
                  </span>
                  <h2 class={`${TIERS[tierIndex(c.count)]} sky-name font-display font-light transition-colors`}>
                    {c.name}
                  </h2>
                </div>
                {/* Rendered, not printed — this row exists to be judged against
                    the real home page, and a bench showing `**` where the site
                    shows bold is a bench lying about its own subject. */}
                {c.description && (
                  <div
                    class="cn-desc text-base-content/55 mt-3 ml-6 max-w-xl font-serif text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed"
                    set:html={renderMarkdown(c.description)}
                  />
                )}
                <span class="sky-cta" aria-hidden="true">
                  <span class="sky-cta__word">Explore</span>
                  <span class="sky-cta__arrow">→</span>
                </span>
              </a>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    )
  }

  {
    /* ─────────  THE FOOTER  ─────────
      ONE set of markup, three arrangements, all in CSS. The content is settled
      (see the header) so the variants are not different footers — they are the
      same six things sitting differently, which is the only honest way to ask
      "which arrangement" without accidentally also changing what is in it.

      ⚠ NO `return`, AND NOTHING THAT RESOLVES. Four variants that closed the
      page were deleted to get here; a footer is where the addresses live. */
  }
  <footer class="hx-foot">
    <div class="hx-foot__inner">
      {/* The destinations. Renamed: Home → Constellations. */}
      <nav class="hx-foot__nav" aria-label="Site">
        {
          SITE_NAV.map((n) => (
            <a href={n.href} data-current={n.room === room ? '' : undefined}>
              {n.label}
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </nav>

      {
        /* The instruments. "Say hello" carries no icon and the other two do —
          which is the brief, and it also happens to be the honest split: the
          first is a sentence you read, the other two are marks you recognise.
          ⚠ THE ⌗ IS A <button>, NOT A LINK, and it targets the SHIPPED popover
          by id. Same drawer as the bar's, opened from the other end of the
          page; the anchoring bug that follows from that is in the script at the
          bottom of this file and in the header notes. */
      }
      <div class="hx-foot__util">
        <a href="/about#mv-hello">Say hello</a>
        <a href="/rss.xml" class="hx-foot__marked">
          <Icon name="ph:rss-simple" class="hx-foot__icon" />
          RSS
        </a>
        <button
          type="button"
          popovertarget="site-colophon"
          data-hx-colophon
          class="hx-foot__marked"
          aria-label="How this site was built"
        >
          <Icon name="simple-icons:github" class="hx-foot__icon" />
          GitHub
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </footer>

  {room === 'blog' && <Reader />}

  {
    /* Bench chrome — fixed, bottom-left, deliberately utilitarian so it can
      never be mistaken for something the site has.

      ⚠ IT FOLDS, AND IT HAS TO SINCE THE FOOTER ARRIVED. Three fieldsets and a
      picker is ~480px of fixed panel pinned to the bottom-left, which is
      exactly where a footer's first column lands — the control was covering the
      only thing half this bench exists to judge. `<details>` rather than a
      move: every other corner is spoken for at some width (the ✦ and
      back-to-top at bottom-right, the theme popover top-right), and folding is
      the one answer that costs nothing when it is not needed. */
  }
  <details class="hx-panel" open>
    <summary class="hx-panel__summary">bench</summary>
    <form class="hx-panel__form" data-hx-panel aria-label="Bench controls">
      {
        /* No control for the bar — it is decided. `?v=current` is still honoured
          by the inline script for a last look at the shipped one. */
      }
      <fieldset class="hx-panel__set">
        <legend class="hx-panel__legend">the bar</legend>
        <label class="hx-panel__row">
          <input type="checkbox" name="sticky" checked={unsticky} />
          unpin it <span class="hx-panel__hint">(scroll away)</span>
        </label>
        <label class="hx-panel__row">
          <input type="checkbox" name="align" checked={measured} />
          align to the measure <span class="hx-panel__hint">(then switch rooms)</span>
        </label>
      </fieldset>

      <fieldset class="hx-panel__set">
        <legend class="hx-panel__legend">the footer</legend>
        {
          FOOTERS.map(([value, label]) => (
            <label class="hx-panel__row">
              <input type="radio" name="f" value={value} checked={value === footer} />
              {label}
            </label>
          ))
        }
      </fieldset>

      <label class="hx-panel__set">
        <span class="hx-panel__legend">room</span>
        <select name="room" data-hx-room>
          <option value="blog" selected={room === 'blog'}>the blog — two mastheads</option>
          <option value="sky" selected={room === 'sky'}>the sky — none at all</option>
        </select>
      </label>
    </form>
  </details>
</SiteLayout>

{
  /* is:global because every selector reaches into SiteLayout's DOM, which
  Astro's scoping cannot see from a page. Safe by construction: this file is the
  only page that loads this stylesheet, and every rule is gated behind a
  `data-hx` attribute that only this page's script ever sets. */
}
<style is:global>
  /* ── shared by the three proposals ────────────────────────────────────────
     The strip stops being a band. Dropping the hairline is the load-bearing
     move (the page's own rule becomes the only one on screen); dropping the
     blur is the second, because a translucent frosted strip is itself one of
     the tells §3 lists. The ground goes SOLID rather than transparent — a
     sticky header still has to hide what passes under it, and "no background"
     is a different proposal (an overlay) that nothing here is making. */
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-bar {
    border-bottom: 0;
    background: var(--color-base-100);
    backdrop-filter: none;
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
  }

  /* No accent in the bar — §5, and the one rule every deleted variant also
     agreed on. Ink alone marks the active item; the PAGE's switch keeps the
     lamplight underline, so "where you are" is said once, loudly, below. */
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-nav > a[aria-current='page'] {
    border-bottom-width: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    color: var(--color-base-content);
  }

  /* ── runninghead ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One register for the whole line. The site's name is not a logo here, it is
     the first item of a running head — same face, same size, same small caps as
     the links beside it, distinguished only by ink. This is the §7 VOICE
     register (serif, all-small-caps, muted, gently tracked) applied to chrome,
     which is a claim worth arguing with: §7 assigns voice to the evocative
     rooms and sans to the machinery ones, and the top bar spans both. It is
     here because the bar is identity before it is instrument. */
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-bar {
    height: 3.25rem;
  }
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-name,
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-nav > a {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  [data-hx='runninghead'] .hx-name {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 80%, transparent);
  }

  /* ── the two toggles ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     `measure` constrains the STRIP, not an inner wrapper — which is only
     equivalent because all three variants have already given up their border
     and their translucency. On `current` it would visibly clip the hairline,
     and that is a fair thing to see. */
  [data-hx-sticky='off'] .hx-bar {
    position: static;
  }
  /* ⚠ `width: 100%` IS LOAD-BEARING, and leaving it out is a silent trap worth
     recording: SiteLayout's wrapper is a COLUMN flex container, so the header is
     a flex item stretched to full width by `align-items: stretch` — and the
     moment an inline `auto` margin appears, auto margins win over stretch and
     the item collapses to its own max-content width. Measured before the fix:
     630px, centred, so the bar was tracking the length of its own text. */
  [data-hx-align='measure'] .hx-bar {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* ── the footer ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     ONE set of markup, three arrangements. Hidden wholesale on `none`, then the
     variant decides how `__nav` and `__util` sit — nothing here changes WHAT is
     in the footer, which is the point of the current round.

     The measure is `max-w-6xl` in BOTH rooms on purpose: a site footer is one
     element, and the sky's column is `max-w-4xl`, so the overhang you see there
     is the same trap the `align` toggle sets at the other end of the page. A
     finding, not a bug — the site has two measures and its chrome has to pick.

     ⚠ THE RULE GOES ON `__inner`'s PSEUDO, NOT ON `__inner`. `__inner` is the
     page's container (`max-w-6xl px-5`), so a border on it draws at the
     container's edge — outside the padding, 20px wider each side than every
     other rule on the page, all of which sit on elements inside it. A footer
     whose one line does not line up with the switch's line above it looks
     bolted on. The pseudo is a grid/flex item at the padded width instead. */
  .hx-foot {
    display: none;
  }
  [data-hx-f='row'] .hx-foot,
  [data-hx-f='stack'] .hx-foot,
  [data-hx-f='chrome'] .hx-foot {
    display: block;
  }
  .hx-foot__inner {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: 1.25rem;
    margin-top: 6rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 0.9rem 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
  }
  .hx-foot__inner::before {
    content: '';
    flex: 1 0 100%;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  /* Both groups, in the bar's own language by default: serif small-caps, the
     §7 VOICE register. `chrome` is the variant that disagrees. */
  .hx-foot__nav,
  .hx-foot__util {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.6rem;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .hx-foot__nav a,
  .hx-foot__util a,
  .hx-foot__util button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 55%, transparent);
    transition: color 150ms;
    cursor: pointer;
    /* ⚠ `text-transform: inherit` IS NOT REDUNDANT. Tailwind's preflight gives
       form elements `font: inherit`, so the button picks up the serif and the
       small caps — but text-transform is not part of the `font` shorthand, and
       the UA stylesheet's `button { text-transform: none }` therefore beats
       inheritance. Under `chrome` that shipped as "SAY HELLO · RSS · GitHub",
       one control quietly refusing the register its two neighbours took. */
    text-transform: inherit;
  }
  .hx-foot__nav a:hover,
  .hx-foot__util a:hover,
  .hx-foot__util button:hover,
  .hx-foot__nav a[data-current] {
    color: var(--color-base-content);
  }
  /* ⚠ SIZED IN `em`, so one rule serves both registers. At small-caps 1rem the
     mark reads at 14px; when `chrome` drops the utilities to 0.72rem the icon
     follows it down instead of towering over its own label. */
  .hx-foot__icon {
    width: 1.05em;
    height: 1.05em;
    /* Small-caps sit on the baseline with a cap height well under the em box,
       so an icon centred on the em box floats. This drops it onto the letters. */
    transform: translateY(0.05em);
  }

  /* stack — two lines, flush left. The nav takes the full row so the utilities
     wrap under it; nothing else changes, which is exactly the comparison. */
  [data-hx-f='stack'] .hx-foot__inner {
    justify-content: flex-start;
    gap: 1.1rem 3rem;
  }
  [data-hx-f='stack'] .hx-foot__nav {
    flex: 1 0 100%;
  }

  /* chrome — the utilities become instruments (§7's chrome register) while the
     destinations stay voice. The claim under test: a link that GOES somewhere
     and a control that DOES something should not be set alike. */
  [data-hx-f='chrome'] .hx-foot__util {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-variant: normal;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    gap: 1.75rem;
  }
  [data-hx-f='chrome'] .hx-foot__util a,
  [data-hx-f='chrome'] .hx-foot__util button {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 45%, transparent);
  }

  /* ⚠ THERE IS NO RULE HERE THAT TOUCHES THE ⌗, and its absence is a decision.
     A `[data-hx-f='colophon'] button[popovertarget='site-colophon']{display:none}`
     stood here for an hour, hiding the colophon trigger whenever the footer took
     the end matter. Michael settled it: the ⌗ stays in the bar in every variant,
     because it is not end matter — it is the site saying, at a glance and before
     anything is opened, that a person built this. Nothing in this stylesheet
     alters the shipped bar's utilities now. */

  /* ── the panel ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  .hx-panel {
    position: fixed;
    bottom: 1rem;
    left: 1rem;
    z-index: 40;
    width: 16rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-200) 92%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--radius-box);
    backdrop-filter: blur(6px);
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 75%, transparent);
  }
  .hx-panel:not([open]) {
    width: auto;
  }
  .hx-panel__summary {
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.95rem;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 45%, transparent);
    user-select: none;
  }
  .hx-panel__summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }
  .hx-panel__form {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 0.95rem 0.85rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
  }
  .hx-panel__set {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.2rem;
    border: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  .hx-panel__legend {
    padding: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    font-variant: all-small-caps;
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 45%, transparent);
  }
  .hx-panel__row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.45rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .hx-panel__hint {
    color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-base-content) 38%, transparent);
  }
  .hx-panel select {
    width: 100%;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    background: var(--color-base-100);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--radius-field);
    padding: 0.15rem 0.3rem;
    color: inherit;
    font: inherit;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /* Attribute-swapping on <html>, so the page under the bar never re-renders
     and a variant change moves ONLY the chrome — the same reason page-card
     swaps one class on one wrapper. The URL is kept in step (replaceState, not
     a navigation) so a reload, or the room picker below, keeps what you were
     looking at. */
  const panel = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>('[data-hx-panel]');
  const root = document.documentElement;

  function apply() {
    if (!panel) return;
    const data = new FormData(panel);
    const f = String(data.get('f') ?? 'none');
    const unpinned = data.get('sticky') !== null;
    const measured = data.get('align') !== null;

    root.dataset.hxF = f;
    if (unpinned) root.dataset.hxSticky = 'off';
    else delete root.dataset.hxSticky;
    if (measured) root.dataset.hxAlign = 'measure';
    else delete root.dataset.hxAlign;

    const p = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
    p.set('f', f);
    unpinned ? p.set('sticky', 'off') : p.delete('sticky');
    measured ? p.set('align', 'measure') : p.delete('align');
    /* `v` is not written — the bar has no control any more, so whatever the URL
       carries (nothing, or the `current` escape hatch) survives a panel change
       instead of being reset by it. */
    history.replaceState(null, '', `${location.pathname}?${p}`);
  }

  panel?.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
    /* The room picker is navigation, not an attribute — the two rooms are two
       different queries. `apply()` first, so the URL it writes is the one the
       reload reads back. */
    if ((e.target as HTMLElement)?.dataset.hxRoom !== undefined) {
      apply();
      const p = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
      p.set('room', String(new FormData(panel).get('room')));
      location.search = `?${p}`;
      return;
    }
    apply();
  });

  /* ── the footer's ⌗, and the shipped bug it exposes ─────────────────────────
     One popover, two triggers — the platform allows that natively, and the
     drawer does open. What does not work is WHERE: `Colophon.astro`'s
     positioning script anchors to

         document.querySelector('button[popovertarget="site-colophon"]')

     which is the FIRST such button in the document, always the top bar's. Press
     the footer's and the panel is placed under a button two thousand pixels
     above, i.e. off-screen. That is a real defect in shipped code that only a
     second trigger could ever have revealed, and the fix is one line there:
     anchor to the button that was PRESSED, not to the first one on the page.

     This re-runs the same maths against the right anchor so the idea can be
     judged at all. ⚠ A DOUBLE rAF, not one: the shipped handler already queued
     its own rAF from this same click, and a single one here would race it —
     two frames out is after it, deterministically, without a timeout's guess. */
  document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    if (!(e.target as HTMLElement)?.closest?.('[data-hx-colophon]')) return;
    const btn = (e.target as HTMLElement).closest('[data-hx-colophon]') as HTMLElement;
    requestAnimationFrame(() =>
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        const pop = document.getElementById('site-colophon');
        if (!pop || !pop.matches(':popover-open')) return;
        const r = btn.getBoundingClientRect();
        const pad = 8;
        const pw = pop.offsetWidth;
        const ph = pop.offsetHeight;
        // Open UPWARD from a footer trigger when there is room, which there
        // always is at the bottom of a page — the shipped script's own flip.
        const above = r.top - ph - pad;
        pop.style.left = `${Math.max(pad, Math.min(r.right - pw, window.innerWidth - pw - pad))}px`;
        pop.style.top = `${above >= pad ? above : Math.max(pad, r.bottom + pad)}px`;
        pop.style.right = 'auto';
        pop.style.bottom = 'auto';
      }),
    );
  });
</script>
